{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017s6e9GkzZMfcwLWvK33yvB","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tI have the full context — the five book-outline stages, the thirteen published cross-bearings pointing into Chapter 9 (two of them section-pinned), Chapter 8's Voyage Ahead handoff, and the cached networking source set. Here is `outline.md` for Chapter 9.\n2\t\n3\t---\n4\t\n5\t```markdown\n6\t---\n7\tchapter: 9\n8\tchapter_type: \"content\"\n9\ttitle: \"Every Pod Has an Address\"\n10\tsubtitle: \"Flat networks, stable names, and the abstraction that survives churn\"\n11\texam_domain: \"Container Orchestration (competency: Networking)\"\n12\tdomain_weight_pct: 7\n13\tcomplexity: \"mixed\"\n14\tnovelty: \"moderate\"\n15\tprereq_factor: \"standard\"\n16\t\n17\t#-- SUBTITLE NOTE. The arc outline's working subtitle is \"Flat networks,\n18\t#-- stable names, and the abstraction that makes churn survivable\" —\n19\t#-- eleven words against this stage's ≤10-word constraint. Tightened\n20\t#-- above to ten by collapsing \"makes churn survivable\" to \"survives\n21\t#-- churn\". All three ideas intact, and the shorter verb lands harder.\n22\t#-- See § Open questions #1.\n23\t\n24\t#-- NOVELTY NOTE. Chapter-level label is `moderate`, but §1 alone is\n25\t#-- genuinely paradigm-shifting for the reader this book is written for.\n26\t#-- An ops professional arrives knowing that machines sit behind NAT,\n27\t#-- that ports get mapped, that reaching a process means reaching a host\n28\t#-- and then a port on it. §1 tells them none of that is true here: every\n29\t#-- Pod is directly routable from every other Pod, no NAT, no proxies.\n30\t#-- That is a model replacement, not a model extension. Drafting should\n31\t#-- treat §1 with the arousal budget of a paradigm shift even though the\n32\t#-- chapter as a whole does not warrant the label.\n33\t\n34\t#-- PREREQ NOTE. `standard`, not `heavy`. B2 calls D2.1 \"the most\n35\t#-- prerequisite-hungry of D2's four competencies\", but every one of\n36\t#-- those prerequisites is internal and recent — Ch 4 (selectors),\n37\t#-- Ch 5 (Pod network namespace), Ch 6 (churn under controllers). No\n38\t#-- external knowledge is assumed beyond ordinary networking literacy.\n39\t\n40\t#-- Section plan (no word budgets) ---------------------------------------\n41\t#-- Length is content-driven. Arc-outline depth band: \"substantial\" — 7\n42\t#-- points, largest of D2's competencies. Planning signal only, NOT a\n43\t#-- target.\n44\t#--\n45\t#-- ⚠ SECTION NUMBERING IS LOAD-BEARING. Thirteen published cross-bearings\n46\t#-- point into this chapter — more than at any chapter so far — and TWO\n47\t#-- of them name a section by number:\n48\t#--   chapter-02 line 600 → *[cross-bearing: see Ch 9 §1 — CNI and pod networking]*\n49\t#--   chapter-05 line 858 → *[cross-bearing: see Ch 9 §4 — readiness and Service endpoint membership]*\n50\t#-- §1 and §4 below honour those exactly. Do not renumber without editing\n51\t#-- chapter-02 and chapter-05. Verified 2026-08-24 against chapters 02-08.\n52\tsections:\n53\t  - name: \"Four Rules and a Plugin\"\n54\t    objectives: [\"D2.1\"]\n55\t    requires_figure: true\n56\t    figure_anchor: \"ch09-fig01-network-model-four-rules\"\n57\t    checkpoint_after: false\n58\t  - name: \"The Address That Doesn't Last\"\n59\t    objectives: [\"D2.1\"]\n60\t    requires_figure: false\n61\t    figure_anchor: null\n62\t    checkpoint_after: false\n63\t  - name: \"Four Ways to Be Reachable\"\n64\t    objectives: [\"D2.1\"]\n65\t    requires_figure: true\n66\t    figure_anchor: \"ch09-fig02-service-types-ladder\"\n67\t    checkpoint_after: true\n68\t  - name: \"The List Behind the Name\"\n69\t    objectives: [\"D2.1\"]\n70\t    requires_figure: true\n71\t    figure_anchor: \"ch09-fig03-service-endpointslice-selector-path\"\n72\t    checkpoint_after: false\n73\t  - name: \"When You Don't Want a Single Address\"\n74\t    objectives: [\"D2.1\"]\n75\t    requires_figure: false\n76\t    figure_anchor: null\n77\t    checkpoint_after: true\n78\t  - name: \"The Component That Makes It Real\"\n79\t    objectives: [\"D2.1\"]\n80\t    requires_figure: true\n81\t    figure_anchor: \"ch09-fig04-kube-proxy-modes\"\n82\t    checkpoint_after: false\n83\t  - name: \"Names, and Where They Resolve\"\n84\t    objectives: [\"D2.1\"]\n85\t    requires_figure: true\n86\t    figure_anchor: \"ch09-fig05-dns-record-shapes\"\n87\t    checkpoint_after: true\n88\t  - name: \"A Query With a Name\"\n89\t    objectives: [\"D2.1\"]\n90\t    requires_figure: true\n91\t    figure_anchor: \"ch09-zenith-stable-name-over-churn\"\n92\t    checkpoint_after: false\n93\t\n94\t#-- Eight sections, matching Chapter 8's count for two more points. The\n95\t#-- shape is different, though: Chapter 8 had four unrelated arcs held\n96\t#-- together by a spine. This chapter has ONE arc — a packet's question,\n97\t#-- \"how do I reach that\" — asked at eight increasing levels of\n98\t#-- resolution. Fold options considered and rejected in § Open questions #10.\n99\t\n100\t#-- Skill v5.3 Part 11: Soundings pre-chapter diagnostic ------------------\n101\tsoundings_planned:\n102\t  question_count: 8\n103\t  topics:\n104\t    - \"three interchangeable copies of a service, one replaced with a new address — what a client needs so it doesn't have to be told\"\n105\t    - \"retrieval from Ch 5 — two containers in one Pod, both wanting port 8080, and how either reaches the other\"\n106\t    - \"retrieval from Ch 6 — a rolling update replaces every Pod; what happens to a client holding one of the old addresses\"\n107\t    - \"retrieval from Ch 4 and Ch 6 — a selector as a query over labels, and what a second controller reading the same labels would be doing\"\n108\t    - \"retrieval from Ch 4 — the Service DNS name form Ch 4 gave in one sentence, and what a bare name does across a namespace boundary\"\n109\t    - \"NAT and address translation in ordinary networks — what the receiving 