{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RhpMkj72Zoz7E7LfWW3c82","type":"tool_result","content":"38:[Omitted long matching line]\n181:| Ingress | An API exposing **HTTP and HTTPS** routes from outside the cluster to Services inside it. |\n182:| Ingress controller | The component that actually fulfills an Ingress; creating an Ingress with no controller has **no effect**. |\n183:| Ingress capabilities | Externally-reachable URLs, load balancing, TLS/SSL termination, name-based virtual hosting, simple fanout by URI. |\n184:| Ingress is frozen | Generally available and covered by GA stability guarantees, but no longer developed; the project recommends **Gateway** instead. |\n185:| Gateway API | The successor to Ingress; dynamic infrastructure provisioning and advanced traffic routing. |\n187:| NetworkPolicy | Built-in API controlling L3/L4 traffic between Pods and between Pods and the outside world. |\n210:| NetworkPolicy as security control | Pod-scoped L3/L4 segmentation — the built-in mechanism for restricting east-west traffic. |\n451:| Ingress · Ingress controller | Service (ClusterIP), HTTP routing, controller pattern |\n452:| Gateway API | Ingress (as the thing it supersedes) |\n453:| NetworkPolicy | Pod, label selector, Namespace, network model, CNI plugin support |\n488:5. **Service must precede Ingress must precede Gateway API.** A strict chain, and the frozen-Ingress fact only lands once the reader knows what Ingress does.\n489:6. **NetworkPolicy sits in both D2.1 and D2.2.** Teach it once, in Networking, and cross-bear from the Security chapter. Duplicating it costs words and creates two slightly-divergent explanations.\n550:| 42 | \"Creating an Ingress object exposes the app.\" | Without an **Ingress controller**, creating an Ingress resource has **no effect**. | [source] |\n551:| 43 | \"Ingress can expose any protocol.\" | Ingress handles **HTTP and HTTPS only**. Other protocols need NodePort or LoadBalancer. | [source] |\n552:| 44 | \"Ingress is deprecated / being removed.\" | It is **frozen**, not deprecated: GA, covered by stability guarantees, with no plans for removal — but no further development. The project *recommends* Gateway for new work. Both halves matter. | [source] |\n553:| 45 | \"All Ingress controllers behave identically.\" | Ideally all fit the reference spec; in reality they operate slightly differently. | [source] |\n555:| 47 | \"Creating a NetworkPolicy secures the cluster.\" | Policies are implemented **by the network plugin**. With a CNI that doesn't support NetworkPolicy, the resource has **no effect** — a silent security failure. | [source] |\n556:| 48 | \"A Pod with no NetworkPolicy is closed by default.\" | Default is **non-isolated** in both directions: all ingress and all egress allowed. | [source] |\n557:| 49 | \"One NetworkPolicy can deny traffic another allows.\" | Policies are **purely additive and never conflict**. There is no deny rule. | [source] |\n559:| 51 | Assuming NetworkPolicy can block node-local traffic or self-traffic. | Traffic to and from the node where a Pod runs is **always allowed**, and a Pod cannot block access to itself. | [source] |\n560:| 52 | Expecting NetworkPolicy to do TLS, name-based service targeting, logging, or explicit deny. | All explicitly out of scope for the current NetworkPolicy API. | [source] |\n673:| G25 | **Gateway API detail.** Recommended over Ingress by the project, but the cached sources give it one sentence. Given that recommendation, the exam may test its existence and basic role. | D2.1 | gateway-api.sigs.k8s.io |"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"737bec61-26cb-41a1-9e4d-42094b6c400b","uuid":"6eae8050-4a18-45b1-9e7a-a00a79644e82","timestamp":"2026-08-25T03:30:48.938Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"38:[Omitted long matching line]\n181:| Ingress | An API exposing **HTTP and HTTPS** routes from outside the cluster to Services inside it. |\n182:| Ingress c