{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RJsGDJfNMZrxbdoVqZDmp4","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tchapter: 9\n3\tchapter_type: \"content\"\n4\ttitle: \"Every Pod Has an Address\"\n5\tsubtitle: \"Flat networks, stable names, and the abstraction that survives churn\"\n6\texam_domain: \"Container Orchestration (competency: Networking)\"\n7\tdomain_weight_pct: 7\n8\tcomplexity: \"mixed\"\n9\tnovelty: \"moderate\"\n10\tprereq_factor: \"standard\"\n11\t\n12\t#-- SUBTITLE NOTE. The arc outline's working subtitle is \"Flat networks,\n13\t#-- stable names, and the abstraction that makes churn survivable\" —\n14\t#-- eleven words against this stage's ≤10-word constraint. Tightened\n15\t#-- above to ten by collapsing \"makes churn survivable\" to \"survives\n16\t#-- churn\". All three ideas intact, and the shorter verb lands harder.\n17\t#-- See § Open questions #1.\n18\t\n19\t#-- NOVELTY NOTE. Chapter-level label is `moderate`, but §1 alone is\n20\t#-- genuinely paradigm-shifting for the reader this book is written for.\n21\t#-- An ops professional arrives knowing that machines sit behind NAT,\n22\t#-- that ports get mapped, that reaching a process means reaching a host\n23\t#-- and then a port on it. §1 tells them none of that is true here: every\n24\t#-- Pod is directly routable from every other Pod, no NAT, no proxies.\n25\t#-- That is a model replacement, not a model extension. Drafting should\n26\t#-- treat §1 with the arousal budget of a paradigm shift even though the\n27\t#-- chapter as a whole does not warrant the label.\n28\t\n29\t#-- PREREQ NOTE. `standard`, not `heavy`. B2 calls D2.1 \"the most\n30\t#-- prerequisite-hungry of D2's four competencies\", but every one of"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"737bec61-26cb-41a1-9e4d-42094b6c400b","uuid":"7f18ffef-7a66-4421-b8ba-9ddb1fe231bb","timestamp":"2026-08-25T03:32:28.954Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\chapter-09-every-pod-has-an-address.md","content":"---\nchapter: 9\nchapter_type: \"content\"\ntitle: \"Every Pod Has an Address\"\nsubtitle: \"Flat networks, stable names, and the abstraction that survives churn\"\nexam_domain: \"Container Orchestration (competency: Networking)\"\ndomain_weight_pct: 7\ncomplexity: \"mixed\"\nnovelty: \"moderate\"\nprereq_factor: \"standard\"\n\n#-- SUBTITLE NOTE. The arc outline's working subtitle is \"Flat networks,\n#-- stable names, and the abstraction that makes churn survivable\" —\n#-- eleven words against this stage's ≤10-word constraint. Tightened\n#-- above to ten by collapsing \"makes churn survivable\" to \"survives\n#-- churn\". All three ideas intact, and the shorter verb lands harder.\n#-- See § Open questions #1.\n\n#-- NOVELTY NOTE. Chapter-level label is `moderate`, but §1 alone is\n#-- genuinely paradigm-shifting for the reader this book is written for.\n#-- An ops professional arrives knowing that machines sit behind NAT,\n#-- that ports get mapped, that reaching a process means reaching a host\n#-- and then a port on it. §1 tells them none of that is true here: every\n#-- Pod is directly routable from every other Pod, no NAT, no proxies.\n#-- That is a model replacement, not a model extension. Drafting should\n#-- treat §1 with the arousal budget of a paradigm shift even though the\n#-- chapter as a whole does not warrant the label.\n\n#-- PREREQ NOTE. `standard`, not `heavy`. B2 calls D2.1 \"the most\n#-- prerequisite-hungry of D2's four competencies\", but every one of","numLines":30,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1713}}}
